namespace Gameworld.Domain;

// The looks and the lore of the default races: the parts "Appearance" and "History and lore" of docs/races-basis.md.
// A kind that has no text here gets DefaultTexts.Missing.

public static partial class DefaultTexts
{
    internal static readonly Dictionary<string, (string Description, string Lore)> Races = new()
    {
        ["northmen"] = (
            "Tall, long in the leg and heavy in the shoulder. The skin is fair and burns before it browns. Hair runs from flaxen through red to light brown and is worn long and combed daily. Men braid their beards. Eyes are blue, grey or green. They dress in wool and linen under a fur-lined cloak pinned at one shoulder, and wear their wealth as silver arm-rings that are cut up when a payment falls due.",
            "Before the Ashen Years the Northmen were forest farmers and fishers at the cold edge of the ploughed world, living on rye, barley, cattle and herring. They call the Ashen Years the Great Winter: three winters with no summer between them, and four more years of bad hay. A farm that fed ten now fed four. The younger sons took the boats that had been built for fishing and went to find out who still had grain. Some traded furs, amber and walrus ivory for it. Some took it. Most did both on the same voyage, and the choice depended on how well the town was walled.\n\nThe hunger ended, and the sea-road did not. Their shallow clinker-built ships can cross open water, run up a river and be dragged over a watershed, so Northmen turned up far inland as traders, settlers of empty coasts, and hired guards of kings who trusted no one born nearby.\n\nThey hold that the world is the upturned hull of a ship, built by the first Shipwright: the sky is its planking and the stars are the rivets. One day it will be righted and launched, and those who died well will row it. A person who dies in bed can still earn a bench by being remembered, which is why they carve stones for kin who died abroad and why a good poet eats at the high table."),
        ["midlanders"] = (
            "Middling in height and build, and mixed in colouring: skin from fair to olive, hair from blond to black, straight or curled, eyes of any colour. A crowd of Midlanders looks like several peoples, which it once was. Rank shows in the clothes. Peasants wear undyed wool and wooden shoes. Townsfolk wear dyed cloth cut to the fashion of their guild. The nobility wear fur trim, long sleeves and a coat of arms.",
            "The Midlanders are the people of the old provinces. When an Old Realm ruled the temperate plains, their ancestors were its farmers, soldiers and clerks, drawn from many conquered tribes and taught one script and one law. When the Realm broke in the Ashen Years they stayed where they were. The roads were still there, and the bridges, the boundary stones and the habit of writing things down. The sorcery was gone. What grew over the ruins was smaller and tougher: a lord with a wooden tower and a dozen horsemen, who protected the villages in his sight and took a share of their harvest for it.\n\nEverything since has been built up from that bargain. Lords swore to greater lords, and the greatest called themselves kings. Each Midlander kingdom claims to be the true successor of the Realm and keeps a little of its ceremony: a crown, an anointing, a court language no peasant speaks. Their histories are full of the wish to put the Realm back together and the wars fought over who would hold it.\n\nThe Midlanders are also the people of the heavy plough. Eight oxen and an iron share turn the wet clay that lighter ploughs cannot, and that opened the forest lowlands to grain. Their saying is that land unploughed is land unfinished. The Elves have another word for it."),
        ["saltroadFolk"] = (
            "Lean, fine-boned and long in the limb, built to shed heat. The skin runs from light brown at the cooler coasts to a deep red-brown in the far desert and the highlands. Hair is black or dark brown, wavy to tightly curled. Eyes are dark brown, now and then green or amber. They wear loose robes in layers and a long cloth wound as turban and veil. Among the desert clans it is the men who veil their faces, and a man of standing is never seen without it.",
            "The Saltroad folk say their cities are older than the Old Realms, and the ruins bear them out. Along the one great river of a desert country their ancestors built in stone before anyone else did, and counted years by the river's flood. When the sorcerer-kings rose elsewhere, the desert was too wide to be worth conquering and too hard to cross without a guide. That has been the fortune of the Saltroad folk ever since: they live on land nobody else can use, and they own the way across it.\n\nThe way is the salt road. South of the desert lie the gold fields of the Sunlanders, where salt is scarce enough to be weighed against gold. North of it are the coasts that want gold. Between them are forty days of sand and a chain of wells that only the desert clans can find. A caravan of a thousand camels carries slabs of salt south and comes back with gold, ivory and kola nuts, and every oasis on the route takes its fee.\n\nIn the Ashen Years the desert suffered least, since it had no harvest to lose. Scholars fled to the oasis towns with their books, and the towns have been places of learning since. A Saltroad library is a family's treasure, walled into the house and passed down. They follow one unseen god, whose law is written, and whose judges are the scholars. In the dry highlands an older rite survives, with priest-kings and temples cut down into the living rock."),
        ["sunlanders"] = (
            "The Sunlanders vary more in build than any other human race. The cattle herders of the open grass are among the tallest people in the world, slender and long-legged. The farmers of the forest edge are of middling height and strongly built, and the hunters of the deep forest are short and light. The skin runs from dark brown to a black with a blue sheen. Hair is black and tightly coiled, and is braided, shaved in patterns or built up with clay and beads to show age, rank and home. Dress is wrapped cotton in strong dyes, indigo first among them.",
            "The Sunlanders hold that they were the first to draw iron from stone, and nobody has proof against it. Their smiths smelted in tall clay furnaces while the Old Realms were still working bronze. A Sunlander smith is more than a craftsman: he commands fire, makes the hoe and the spear, and is feared and honoured like a priest. In many of their kingdoms the first king is remembered as a smith.\n\nIron cleared the forest edge and armed the grassland, and gold paid for the rest. The gold fields lie in Sunlander country, washed from river gravel and dug from shallow pits. The kings who held them grew rich enough that one, on a journey, gave away so much gold that its price fell for a generation. That is the tale the Saltroad folk tell, and the Sunlanders do not deny it.\n\nThe Old Realms never ruled them. A fly of the wet forest kills horses and cattle with a sleeping sickness, and no mounted army could pass it. So the grassland built empires on cavalry, and the forest built walled city-states that no cavalry could reach. In the Ashen Years the rains failed, the herders moved south and east, and the kingdoms they met or founded on the way make up the Sunlander lands today.\n\nLittle of this is written down. It is kept by the praise-singers, a hereditary class who hold the genealogy and deeds of every great family in memory and sing them on demand. A king without a praise-singer has no past."),
        ["grassRiders"] = (
            "Compact, deep-chested and bow-legged from a life on horseback, with broad faces, high cheekbones and narrow dark eyes creased against wind and glare. The skin is tan to bronze and weathered early. Hair is straight and black, and men shave the crown and braid what is left behind the ears. Both sexes wear a long wrapped coat belted at the waist, felt boots and a fur hat, and carry a bow case and a knife.",
            "The steppe cannot be ploughed. It has grass, and what turns grass into a living is the horse, the sheep and the will to move. The Grass riders have lived that way since the Dawn: a few families and their herds, shifting camp four or five times a year, each clan at feud or at marriage with the next.\n\nTheir history has one shape, which repeats. For generations the clans raid each other and the farmers at the steppe's edge, and no one outside pays them much attention. Then a leader appears who can make the clans obey. He breaks up the old clans and sorts the fighting men into tens, hundreds and thousands under officers chosen for merit. He gives one written law for everyone. In a few years he has an army in which every man has three remounts and can shoot from the saddle at a gallop, and no farming kingdom can stand against it. The Old Realms paid tribute to such khans. The Ashen Years, which killed the herds and drove the clans outward together, broke the realms that still stood.\n\nThen the khan dies, his heirs divide the conquests, and within a few generations each is ruling farmers from a palace and is a steppe lord in name only. The clans at home begin again. Every Grass rider knows the shape and believes the next khan will be the one whose realm holds.\n\nThey worship the Sky, which is everywhere and needs no temple. A shaman speaks to the lesser spirits for a fee. They care little what conquered people believe, tax every faith alike and enjoy setting priests to argue before the khan."),
        ["rainkeepers"] = (
            "Slight to middling in height and fine-featured. The skin runs from a light wheat-brown to very dark brown, often within one town. Hair is black, straight or wavy, oiled, and worn long by both sexes. Eyes are large and dark. Dress is unsewn cotton: a length of cloth wrapped and pleated at the waist for a man, a longer one drawn over the shoulder for a woman, in colours that tell the wearer's community. A mark of coloured paste on the brow shows which god the wearer follows.",
            "The monsoon lands get a year's rain in three months and none in the other nine. The Rainkeepers became a people when they learned to hold on to it. They dammed a shallow valley with an earth bank to make a reservoir, then another below it to catch the overflow, and another below that. A chain of reservoirs feeds rice fields through the dry season, and the kingdom that builds the chains can feed cities. Their kings are judged by their dams, and the proudest boast in their inscriptions is that not one drop reaches the sea without first serving the people.\n\nThe Old Realms traded with them and did not conquer them. Their own empires rose and fell on the rain: a run of bad monsoons broke more dynasties than any enemy. The Ashen Years stopped the rain for three seasons. The cities emptied and the forest took the canals. What the Rainkeepers rebuilt afterwards was a land of many middling kingdoms, each around a temple town.\n\nTheir thought runs to great spans of time. They hold that the world is made, destroyed and made again in cycles of millions of years, and that a soul is reborn through many lives, higher or lower by its deeds, until it wins release. They have a thousand gods, whom their teachers call so many faces of one. Their epics are the longest poems in the world. Their scholars gave the world its numerals, the zero and the game of kings."),
        ["canalFolk"] = (
            "Middling in height, slender in the south and sturdier in the north. The skin runs from pale tan to golden-brown. Hair is straight and black, never cut, and worn in a topknot under a cap. Eyes are dark and narrow-lidded. Dress is a robe crossed left over right and tied with a sash. Farmers wear hemp or cotton and a wide straw hat. Officials wear silk in the colour of their grade, with a square badge on the chest that shows their rank to anyone who can read it.",
            "The Canal folk live on river plains that flood. Their first hero was an engineer who tamed the flood by digging channels to lead the water away, after his father had failed with dams. The lesson shaped everything after: water is managed by many people working to one plan for many years, and that needs someone to keep the plan. So they have the oldest continuous state in the world, built to maintain dikes, canals and granaries.\n\nThey call that state the Realm, and they hold that there is only one. Heaven grants its mandate to a ruling house for as long as the house rules well. Floods, famines and rebellions are signs that the mandate has been withdrawn, and whoever then wins the throne has received it. So a dynasty may fall, and the Realm goes on. The Canal folk alone among peoples say that their Old Realm never ended. It lost its sorcery in the Ashen Years and kept its files.\n\nIn the Scattered Age the throne is disputed or empty, and each Canal folk state is ruled by a governor or king who holds it, by his own account, in trust for the emperor to come. All of them keep the same calendar, script, weights and ritual, so that the pieces will fit when they are put back together.\n\nTheir teaching is about conduct, not gods: the duty of child to parent, subject to ruler, younger to elder, and the duty of the ruler to deserve it. Ancestors are fed and informed at the family altar. Other faiths are welcome so long as they do not question the order."),
        ["elf"] = (
            "A head taller than most humans and lighter in build, with long hands and a way of standing entirely still. The ears rise to a point. The face is fine-boned and shows no age after the thirtieth year: an Elf of four hundred has a smooth brow and old eyes. Colouring follows the forest. Elves of the cold woods are pale and silver-haired, those of the oak woods brown-haired and green-eyed, those of the warm forests dark and gold-eyed. They dress in greens, greys and browns that are hard to see between trees.",
            "The Elves say the temperate world was once forest from shore to shore and that they planted a good part of it. It may be so. An Elf thinks nothing of setting an acorn where a hall will be wanted in two hundred years. In the Dawn they tended the woods as a farmer tends a field, but on a rotation of centuries. They thinned and coppiced, moved saplings, bred trees for nuts and fruit, and grew their halls from living trunks.\n\nThen humans came with fire and the plough. The Elves did not at first understand that a people could clear land and never let it grow back. By the time of the Old Realms they understood it well. The Realms cut whole forests for fleets and furnaces, and the Elves fought them in a string of wars that each lasted one human generation and that the Elves count as a single war, not yet over. They also taught the first sorcerer-kings. Elves do not like to be reminded of it.\n\nIn the Ashen Years the old forest lived on its stored strength while the grain fields died. Some elven houses opened their stores of nuts and dried fruit to the starving farmers at the forest edge. Others closed the paths. That choice still divides them into the Open houses and the Closed, and it still decides how each forest is regarded by its neighbours.\n\nThey are few and grow fewer. A couple may have two children in five centuries, and a war that kills fifty is a disaster that takes three hundred years to mend."),
        ["dwarf"] = (
            "Four to four and a half feet tall and nearly as broad, with thick wrists, big hands and a heavy brow. The skin is ruddy or brown and seamed like worked leather. Both men and women grow beards, which are braided and ringed to show clan, craft and standing, so outsiders often cannot tell the sexes apart and Dwarves see no reason to help them. They see well in very little light. They dress in leather and thick wool and are rarely without a tool on the belt.",
            "The Dwarves hold that the first of them were not born. They woke inside the rock and dug their way out, and the urge to dig is the memory of it. They have a sense no other race has: a Dwarf feels the depth, the direction and the grain of the stone around him, and is never lost underground.\n\nThrough the Dawn and the Old Realms they went deeper. A hold begins as a mine and becomes a city: halls, forges, cisterns and tombs cut into a mountain behind one gate. The sorcerer-kings never ruled them, since a hold cannot be taken except by starving it, and the Realms wanted dwarven steel more than dwarven obedience. They paid in grain. For a thousand years the holds grew rich and stopped keeping fields.\n\nThen the Ashen Years came and the grain carts did not. The Dwarves call this the Hungry Gate. Holds that had never been breached starved behind their own doors. Others opened the gate and sent their people out as smiths, masons and soldiers to anyone who could feed them. The holds that came through drew two lessons, which are now law in all of them. Every hold keeps seven years of grain in sealed halls. And every hold keeps treaties with the farmers below it, and honours them to the grain."),
        ["halfling"] = (
            "Three to three and a half feet tall, built like small adults, not like children: sturdy, round in the face and inclined to be stout. The hair is thick and curly, brown or sandy. The eyes are bright and the hands are deft. They walk very quietly without trying. They have a sense of taste and smell far finer than a human's and can name the field a cheese came from. They dress in bright waistcoats, wide hats and good boots, and wear their prosperity on their backs.",
            "The small folk have always lived in the gaps the big folk left: river meadows, the hedged corners of the lowlands, the sunny slopes too steep for the heavy plough. They have no tale of where they came from and no interest in one. Their history is family history, kept in genealogies that every Halfling can recite to the seventh cousin.\n\nTheir name is a tax record. The registers of an Old Realm entered each of them at half a head, since they ate half a ration and could carry half a load. The clerks wrote \"halfling\" in the margin and the word stuck. Their own word for themselves just means the Folk.\n\nA Halfling household has always kept a deep cellar, and custom says it should hold enough to feed the family for seven years, though no one remembers who set the number. In the Ashen Years the custom paid. Small bodies need little, the cellars were full, and the Halflings were the one people of the farmland who did not starve. Most of them fed their human neighbours as well. The neighbours have not forgotten. The charters that give Halfling districts their own law inside human kingdoms nearly all date from the first years after the ash."),
        ["gnome"] = (
            "About three feet tall and lightly built, with a large head, a long nose, big ears and very large eyes of a clear grey or brown. Hair goes white early and is worn in tufts or under a pointed cap. The fingers are long and thin. A Gnome sees close detail as a human sees through a lens: the facets of a seed, the scratches on a gear. At thirty paces the same Gnome sees a blur and wears ground glass spectacles to correct it. They dress in many pockets.",
            "The Gnomes are hill folk. Where the Dwarves went into the mountain, the Gnomes dug warrens under the roots of the oak and beech woods in the foothills, shallow, dry and warm. They were never numerous and never fought anyone if there was a back door.\n\nTheir close sight made them the makers of everything fine. In the Old Realms the sorcerer-kings prized them above gold. Gnomes ground the lenses, cut the crystals, engraved the measuring rings and built the clockwork that the sorcery needed, and they were kept in comfortable workshops that had locks on the outside. Being Gnomes, they kept a copy of every design.\n\nWhen the Realms broke, the Gnomes walked out with the drawings and without the masters. They drew one conclusion from the whole age and made it a rule. No workshop takes more than a third of its work from one patron. A Gnome will sell to a king, and will not belong to one.\n\nThey went into their warrens in the Ashen Years with mushroom beds and with something nobody else had thought to save in good order: seed. Sorted, labelled and sealed, the seed vaults of the warrens resowed a good many fields when the sun came back. Farmers still buy their seed from Gnomes when they want it true to kind."),
        ["orc"] = (
            "Six to seven feet tall and heavy in the bone, with long arms, a thick neck, a low brow and two tusks rising from the lower jaw. The skin is grey-green, olive or dun brown. Hair is coarse and black and is worn in a crest or a knot. The eyes are small, yellow or red-brown, and see well at dusk. Scars are left to show and are sometimes rubbed with ash so that they stand out: an Orc's skin is a record. Dress is leather, hide and whatever armour was won.",
            "In the Dawn the Orcs lived on good land too. Humans with ploughs fed ten on the ground that fed one Orc hunter, and over the generations the Orcs were pushed outward to the dry plains, the scrub and the badlands, which is where they are now. The land made them what they are. An Orc can eat carrion, bone, bark and meat a week dead and take no harm, heals from wounds that would kill a human, and is full grown at twelve.\n\nThe Old Realms saw a use for that. They raised orc legions, the heavy infantry of the sorcerer-kings, paid them in loot and settled the veterans in forts along the frontiers. When the Realms fell the garrisons did not go home, because the forts were home. Many orc holds today stand on the walls of an old frontier fort, and their chiefs keep the standard of a legion that no state has paid for a thousand years.\n\nThey have a name for the Ashen Years, and it is the Lean Feast. When the harvests failed, the Orcs lived on what the others could not eat. They came out of the poor land into country emptied by famine and took a good deal of it. The stories that make them monsters date from then: they arrived when everyone was starving, and they were not."),
        ["hoarkin"] = (
            "Stocky and short-limbed, with skin as pale as skimmed milk that shades to blue-grey at the lips, the nails, the eyelids and the tips of the ears. Hair is white or ash-grey, fine and dense. The eyes are pale grey with a dark rim. A Hoarkin never shivers and moves slowly and exactly. Their breath makes no cloud in the frost, and their hand is as cold as a stone from the beach. They wear one layer of sealskin, against wind and wet. They do not need it against cold.",
            "The Hoarkin are cousins of the humans who followed the seals out onto the ice in the Dawn and stayed, for more generations than human history has. A warm body on the ice burns food all day to stay alive. Over that long time the Hoarkin body stopped trying. Something in their blood keeps it liquid far below freezing. At rest the heart beats twenty times a minute. A Hoarkin can sit by a seal's breathing hole from one tide to the next without moving and without harm, and needs a third of the food a human does. That is how the ice can feed a people at all.\n\nTheir own account is a bargain. The First Mother met Cold on the ice, and Cold was killing her children. She gave him their fire, and he agreed to stop. So the Hoarkin have no fire inside them, and they call every other people the Burning: folk who are alight within, eat without end to feed the flame, and die of it young.\n\nIn the Ashen Years the ice spread south and the Hoarkin came with it, further than they had ever been, onto coasts emptied by famine. They call that time the Wide Years. When the sun returned the ice drew back and most of them followed it home. A few bands were stranded on high glaciers, where they still are."),
        ["hearthbloods"] = (
            "Broad, deep-chested and well fleshed, with skin of a flushed copper red, like someone just in from a hard run. Hair is black or dark red and cropped short. The eyes are dark with a ruddy light in them. In frost a Hearthblood walks in a cloud of their own breath, steam rises from the bare shoulders, and snow melts where they sit. They wear a kilt and an open vest in weather that kills. Their touch is as hot as a brow in a fever.",
            "The Hearthbloods are the youngest of the races, and they remember their making. They were a human people of the high valleys. When the Ashen Years came, the snow in the passes did not melt for three summers, and they were shut in. They burned the firewood, then the furniture, then the roof beams. In the third winter the hearth-keeper of the last hall that still had a fire stood over the last coals. The hall stood by a warm spring, in a place where the world is thin and things asked for are sometimes given. She asked that the fire should not go out. Then she stirred the coals into the last broth, and everyone drank.\n\nNobody in that hall was ever cold again, and neither were their children. No Hearthblood hall has had a hearth since. The people are the hearth.\n\nThe price showed within the week. A fire has to be fed. A Hearthblood eats twice what a human eats and is in danger after two days without food. On the fifth day they are dead. They also burn out early, and forty-five is old."),
        ["widegaze"] = (
            "Tall, lean and long-necked, with tawny skin and sand-coloured or brown hair tied back hard, so that it never crosses the eyes. The eyes are set wide, almost at the corners of the face. The iris is pale gold, and the pupil is a dark bar that stays level with the ground however the head is tilted. A Widegaze never seems to look at the person they are talking to, because they have no need to turn towards what they watch. They wear long plain coats and a brimmed hat cut away at the sides.",
            "The Widegaze say plainly that their mothers were hunted. In the Dawn their ancestors lived on open grassland among great cats and running wolves, with no trees to climb and no caves. Those who saw the grass move lived. After enough generations of that, they were a people who could not be surprised: able to see in front, to both sides and most of the way behind in a single glance, and to pick out a rider at the edge of sight.\n\nThey never built a wall. Their safety was to see first and to be gone.\n\nThe Old Realms gave them a second trade. A sorcerer-king's frontier was too long to garrison, so the Realm raised a line of earth mounds across the plain, each within sight of the next, and set a Widegaze family on every one. A message passed by mirror flash and smoke from mound to mound crossed the Realm in an afternoon. The Realm fell, and the line remained. The mounds are now the Widegaze villages, the families still pass messages along them for a fee, and nobody else has ever learned the code. They call the line the Watch.\n\nIn the Ashen Years they were the first on the plain to see the ash coming, as a brown wall on the horizon, three days out. They moved their herds early and lost fewer than anyone."),
        ["amberbloods"] = (
            "Sturdy and broad-handed, with brown-gold skin scattered with freckles the colour of clear resin. The eyes are amber and catch the light like a bead held to the sun. Hair is chestnut, copper or bark-brown. A faint grain, like that of planed wood, runs along the backs of the hands and down the spine, and deepens with age. A cut does not bleed. It wells one golden bead, which hardens in a breath. The old move stiffly, their skin is ridged, and their voices creak.",
            "In the age of the Old Realms the loggers came north. The Realms wanted masts and charcoal, and they cut the great cold forests a valley at a time. The people who lived there were human then: woodcutters, trappers and charcoal burners, paid by the Realm to fell their own home. One winter they stopped.\n\nTheir story says that the forest itself made the offer, through whatever it is that lives in the oldest stands, where the world is thin. Trees cannot walk and cannot fight. The forest needed a people who could. It would give them its blood, so that steel could hardly kill them. In return they would give it their ending: when one of them could walk no longer, they would come back and stand. The people agreed, drove the loggers out over thirty years, and have kept the terms since.\n\nThe Elves say the compact was offered to them first and that they refused the price. The Amberbloods say nobody asked the Elves.\n\nLater ages brought a worse enemy than loggers. Their blood, once set, is the finest varnish and glue there is, staunches any wound, and after some years in the ground becomes true amber. In the Tapping Wars, human slavers took Amberbloods alive to bleed them, a cup a week. They do not forgive it, and the trade in blood amber is not quite dead."),
        ["brinefolk"] = (
            "Spare and smooth-skinned, with a grey-white skin that has the faint sheen of the inside of a shell. Hair is pale and stiff. The eyes are a very light grey or green. Beside each eye lies a gland that sheds the salt they take in, so a Brinefolk weeps brine all day without sorrow. It dries to a white crust on the lashes, in the corners of the eyes and along the brow, which they brush away or shape into ornament as fashion bids. They lick their lips often. They dress in pale wrapped linen, and both sexes line the eyes with dark paint against the glare of the flats.",
            "The Brinefolk are not cousins of the humans, and their oldest carvings say so. The basins of the cold desert once held an inland sea. In its shallows lived a people the carvings show with fins along the arms, who gathered shellfish and never left the water for long. Over the Dawn the sea shrank. Each generation waded in shallower, saltier water than the last, and each stood a little straighter. When the last of the sea had gone into the sky, what was left on the white flats walked on two legs and looked, to a stranger, like a pale kind of human.\n\nThe clerks of the Old Realms listed them as human and taxed them in salt. The Realms needed salt in quantity, to keep meat for their armies and fish for their cities, and the Brinefolk had the pans and the patience. They grew rich on it. In the Ashen Years they grew richer. What little food the world had needed keeping, and for seven years a block of salt bought its weight in silver. Other peoples remember those years with grief, and remember who set the price.\n\nThey believe the sea will come back. The flats are its bed, kept in trust. So they do not plough there or build higher than one storey, and they bury their dead packed in salt, sitting up, so that the dead will be whole and ready when the water returns."),
        ["duskreaders"] = (
            "Slight and narrow-built, with deep brown skin that has a grey undertone. The eyes are large and wholly black, without a visible white or iris, and glossy as wet stone. By day they wear a visor of smoked horn with a slit in it, or a veil of dark gauze. Among themselves they dress in thick quilted robes, gloves and often a mask of stiffened cloth. The robes are not for warmth. They hide the body's heat, and with it everything the body gives away.",
            "In the Dawn their ancestors sheltered from the desert day in deep canyons and caves and came out to hunt at night. Over many generations in the dark, their eyes learned to see warmth: a hare under a bush, a snake on a rock, the ground a herd lay on an hour ago. A cool seep under sand shows dark to them, so they find water where others die.\n\nThe same sight shows them a blush, a fever, a quickened pulse in the throat and the heat of a hand that has just held a hidden knife. The sorcerer-kings of the Old Realms saw the use in it at once. They set Duskreaders beside their thrones as examiners. Subjects called them the King's Eyes, since nobody could stand in front of one and lie calmly. The kings were served, and their examiners were hated. When the Realms fell the mobs went for them first, and the survivors fled back into the deep desert.\n\nOut of that they made the rule every Duskreader child learns: we read, we do not tell. A Duskreader says what they have been hired to see and nothing else, ever. An order of them, the Discreet, takes it as a formal vow.\n\nThe Ashen Years they remember differently from everyone else. The dimmed sun made the desert day mild, and for seven years they walked abroad at noon. They call it the Kind Time, and they have learned not to say so in company."),
        ["noonfolk"] = (
            "Tall, slender and wide in the shoulder. The skin runs from copper-brown to deep olive and carries a tracery of green-bronze lines, most clearly across the shoulders, the back and the upper arms. The lines darken through the day and pale overnight. Noonfolk have no hair at all. The eyes are green or gold, with pupils that shrink to a point in full sun. They wear a wrap at the hips, and nothing that shades the back.",
            "There was a kingdom of the Old Realms in the dry grassland that suffered one famine after another. Its sorcerer-king saw that most of every harvest went to feed the people who grew it. He set his makers to breed field hands who would not need feeding. They took debtors and convicts and worked on them, generation after generation, with a craft borrowed from the leaf. The result was the Noonfolk, who laboured from dawn to dusk on a bowl of gruel and a great deal of water.\n\nThe makers left two flaws. A Noonfolk falls asleep when the sun goes down, wherever they are standing, and nothing wakes them before dawn. So they were useless as soldiers, sentries or house servants. And nobody had planned for a sky without sun. When the Ashen Years came, the made people starved faster than anyone. Their stomachs were too small to live on food alone. Nine in ten died. They call it the Dark Hunger. The kingdom died in the same years, and the Noonfolk who were left walked off the estates and were not stopped.\n\nThey say of it: the king made us to eat light. Nobody asked the Sun. The Sun feeds us anyway."),
        ["boughwalkers"] = (
            "Slender and very light, with arms that reach the knee, long hands, and feet that grip like a second pair of hands. The skin is dark brown to red-brown. Hair is black and braided flat to the skull, so that nothing can catch on it. The eyes are large and hazel-green. A Boughwalker has perfect balance and no fear of a drop. They wear belts, pouches and short wraps of cloth that looks plain brown or white to everyone else. To their own eyes it blazes with pattern.",
            "In the Dawn their ancestors went up into the canopy of the wet forest and stayed. The fruit is there, with the light and the breeze. The forest floor is dark, flooded half the year, and full of fever and things with teeth. Countless generations aloft gave them their reach and grip. It also gave them eyes for the work that matters most up there: telling ripe fruit from unripe, and wholesome from poisonous, among a thousand shades of green. Flowers, fruit, feathers and some poisons show a colour beyond violet. Boughwalkers see it. They call it the high colour and cannot describe it to anyone else.\n\nThe Old Realms never reached them. The Ashen Years did. The canopy thinned, the fruit failed, and for the first time in memory the Boughwalkers came down to the ground to look for food. They call it the Year on the Floor. Many died of the fevers of the floor, and those who lived had met the ground peoples and started to trade with them. One custom has been kept from it. Every young Boughwalker spends a floor-year among the ground peoples before marrying."),
        ["shoalfolk"] = (
            "Lean, sleek and very deep in the chest. The skin is brown with a grey or green cast, smooth and faintly oiled. Hair is black, cropped short or braided with shell. The eyes are large and dark. When a Shoalfolk blinks, a clear membrane flicks sideways across the eye, and it stays shut under water. The nostrils are slits that close at will. They wear a loincloth and a knife, and add a belt of lead weights when diving. Children swim before they walk.",
            "Their ancestors were shore gatherers on the warm tidal coasts, where the mangroves leave no land to farm and the sea is full of food. Generations of diving for it remade them. The lungs and spleen grew and the eyes learned to focus in water. The skin came to need the sea.\n\nThe Old Realms found them useful. Officials set a quota of pearls on every Shoalfolk village and sent the divers deeper each year to fill it. Many came up bleeding from the ears, or did not come up. The Shoalfolk answer was to leave the land altogether. They rebuilt their villages on stilts in the shallows, or on rafts of boats, past the low-water mark where a king's law stops. No ground, no lord, they say, and they have kept to it. To this day a Shoalfolk village pays no land tax, because it stands on none.\n\nIn the Ashen Years the fields failed, and the tide still came in twice a day and brought fish with it. The Shoalfolk ate, and sold dried fish to the coast farmers who had looked down on them. They took the lesson that the land fails and the sea does not, and they do not let the farmers forget it."),
        ["swiftborn"] = (
            "Small, wiry and quick, with warm olive or brown skin that is hot to the touch, and a pulse that can be seen in the throat. The eyes are bright and never still. The hair grows a hand's width in a year, dark in summer and pale in winter, so that it is ringed like a cut tree and an age can be counted off a braid. It is worn long for that reason. A Swiftborn of ten is a grown adult. One of twenty-five has the lined face and white brows of a human of seventy.",
            "Late in the age of the Old Realms a plague emptied a warm and fertile province. The vineyards went wild, the olive harvest fell unpicked, and whole towns stood with their doors open. The sorcerer who governed the province wanted it full again in his own lifetime. His makers took settlers and quickened them. Their children were grown in seven years and had large families of their own, and in thirty years the province was full.\n\nNobody had asked what would happen in the thirty-first. The first quickened generation died of old age, all within a few years of each other, and the governor who had made them watched it. He lived to bury four generations of his people. They say it broke his mind, and that it was the fifth generation that pulled him down.\n\nThe Ashen Years lasted seven years. That is a quarter of a Swiftborn life. A whole generation was born, grew up and had children without once seeing a clear sun, and is remembered as the Ash-born. The Swiftborn came through by numbers alone. They died fast, and were born faster."),
        ["flinchless"] = (
            "Tall, strong and upright. They were bred from prisoners of many peoples, so skin and hair are of every kind. The eyes are always the same, a pale silver-grey, which was the maker's mark. The skin is crossed with thin white scars, and a missing fingertip or a crooked, badly healed bone is common. What unsettles a stranger is what is absent. A Flinchless does not blink at a raised hand, does not favour a hurt leg and does not wince.",
            "A war-wizard of an Old Realm, remembered only as the Marshal, wanted foot soldiers who would not break. He bred them from captives and took the pain out of them. The legion he raised never lost a battle line. It also lost a third of its strength every winter, to wounds nobody had noticed, frostbite nobody had felt, and broken feet that had been marched on for a week.\n\nThe makers could not put the pain back. They solved it with drill. Every soldier was paired with another, and at dawn and dusk each pair stripped and examined one another from scalp to sole. This was called the Looking-over. It worked, and it is the root of everything the Flinchless are.\n\nWhen the Realm fell the legion was left without a master. Part of it marched into the farmland, took up empty holdings and married. Part stayed under arms as companies for hire. Both parts still exist. They divide on one question: whether the oath sworn to the Marshal passed to the kingdoms that claim to be the Realm's heirs. The Oathkept say yes and serve those crowns. The Released say the oath died with him."),
        ["lanternkin"] = (
            "Slight and very pale, with skin so thin that it seems almost clear, and fine hair the colour of ash. The eyes are a light grey-blue. In daylight a Lanternkin looks only sickly. In shadow a blue-green tracery shows at the wrists, the throat and the temples, and it brightens and dims with the heartbeat. The sun burns them quickly, so out of doors they wear a hood, long sleeves and gloves.",
            "Their ancestors lived on the floor of the deepest wet forest, where daylight never reaches, among fungi that glow. It was a place where the world is thin. In a famine in the Dawn they ate the glowing caps, having nothing else. Their children's blood shone. It has shone in every generation since.\n\nFor a long time it was only a way to find each other in the dark. Then a mother noticed that the drop she had kept in a glass bead from each of her children still glowed, year after year, until the one bead that went dark on the night her son drowned three valleys away. That is the lamp, and it changed what the Lanternkin were for.\n\nThe sorcerer-kings were quick to see the use of it. An Old Realm placed a Lanternkin on the staff of every governor and general, and kept the lamps together in a hall in the capital, so that the death of an officer's household was known at court the same hour. Other peoples' blood was tried and did not work.\n\nIn the Ashen Years, when the days were dark at noon, villages of every race took Lanternkin in as the light of the house. Many never went back to the forest. Most Lanternkin have lived in other peoples' towns ever since."),
        ["stillfaces"] = (
            "Of middling height, sturdy and soft-footed, with tawny-grey skin and thick hair brindled in brown and grey. The eyes are dark, round and steady. The nose is broad, with wide nostrils that flare when they attend to something. The face is well made and entirely still. It does not smile, frown or lift a brow, and the mouth moves only to eat and to speak. A row of dark spots, like a string of moles, runs along each side of the jaw and behind the ear. Their voices are level. They stand close to a stranger and breathe in.",
            "The Stillfaces say they were in the forest before the upright peoples came. In their own telling their ancestors went on four feet and spoke entirely by scent. When the first humans walked into the woods, they stood up to meet them, out of courtesy, and stayed standing. The face came last, they say, and they never learned to work it. Scholars who have examined them agree at least that the bones are arranged differently, and that nothing in them is cousin to a human.\n\nThe Old Realms listed them as the mask people and used them as trackers and as finders of poison at court. They left little mark on them.\n\nThe Ashen Years left a deep one. The ash that hid the sun also smothered scent. For seven years the forest smelled of nothing but cinders. To a Stillface that was like going deaf and dumb at once. They call those years the Silence. Villages that had talked across a valley on the wind fell out of touch, families broke, and many went mad. In that need they learned speech in the mouth, properly, from their human neighbours. Their spoken language to this day is a borrowed human one, used flatly, like a tool."),
        ["twofold"] = (
            "Of middling height, graceful and even-featured. At any age an outsider finds it hard to say whether a given Twofold is a man or a woman, and the Twofold are amused by the effort. The skin is warm brown to dark brown. The hair is black and worn long by everyone. In the hollow of the throat lies a small patch of skin, pale gold in the young and deep indigo in the old, which changes during the year of the Turning. Everyone dresses alike, in wrapped cotton. Only the sash differs: saffron before the Turning, indigo after it.",
            "The Twofold tell it like this. Once they were as other peoples are, and their men and women fell into a war with each other that lasted three generations. Each half held its own villages. Children were stolen back and forth across the line. At the end both sides were too few to go on. Both went to the shrine of the Pair, the god with two faces, and each asked for judgement against the other.\n\nThe Pair gave one judgement to both: each of you will be both, and will judge the other as yourself. Since then no Twofold has been only a man or only a woman, and there has been nothing left to have that war about.\n\nThe Old Realms had no column in their registers for them, and in the end taxed them by the hearth. In the Ashen Years many Twofold turned early, some at twenty, as if the body knew that mothers were needed. Their houses refilled within a generation."),
        ["bitterbloods"] = (
            "Lean and smooth-skinned, with a dark olive-brown skin broken by patches of bright colour: sky blue, sulphur yellow, sometimes orange, across the throat, the shoulders and the outer arms. The patches are brightest in those whose blood is strongest, and are worn uncovered with pride at home. Hair is black and slicked back with oil. The eyes are dark with a yellow ring. Among other peoples a Bitterblood wears gloves and a coat of wax on the skin, and carries a cup of their own.",
            "One of the Old Realms lay in the wet tropics, and its court ruled by poison. Every heir came to the throne over the bodies of the others. The dynasty wanted tasters who could not be killed at the table. Over forty generations the court alchemists raised a caste of servants on rising doses of every venom, root and mineral in the kingdom, and worked the tolerance into the blood, so that it was passed to the children.\n\nThey did it too well. The tasters became proof against poison, and then they became poison. A king died of a cup that a taster had sipped from first. A prince died of a kiss. The makers had given the caste its colours so that anyone at court could tell a taster at sight, as a livery worn in the skin. The court now read those colours as a threat, and ordered the caste destroyed.\n\nThe tasters fled into the swamp forest and the mangroves, where the fevers killed the soldiers sent after them and did not touch the fugitives. The dynasty starved in the Ashen Years. The Bitterbloods did not, because they can eat what poisons everyone else: bitter roots raw, toadstools, puffer fish, the frogs of the forest floor. No one has ever competed with them for their food."),
        ["catfolk"] = (
            "Apart from the ears, the tail and the eyes, a Catfolk looks like a human. The skin is human skin, bare and smooth, with no fur on the face or the body. There are no whiskers, no muzzle and no claws: the nose, the mouth, the teeth, the hands and the nails are human. They are lean, a little under middling height, and light on their feet.\n\nThe ears are a cat's ears: upright, pointed and furred, set high on the head. A Catfolk has no other ears, and the sides of the head are smooth under the hair. The ears turn toward a sound. The tail is as long as the leg, furred all over, and never quite still. Ears and tail show what the person feels, and a Catfolk cannot stop them.\n\nThe colouring follows the cat. The skin runs through the colours of a cat's coat: cream, sand, tawny gold, russet, warm brown, smoke grey and near black. The hair is human hair, worn at any length, in black, brown, sand, ginger, grey or white, and often in two of them, streaked or patched. The fur of the ears and the tail matches the hair, sometimes with a darker tip or with rings. The eyes are amber, gold, copper, green or pale blue, brighter than a human's, and now and then one of each. The pupil is a cat's: an upright slit in daylight, wide and round in the dark. They wear light, loose clothes cut to leave the tail free, and nothing that covers the ears.",
            "The Catfolk were human once. They were the granary keepers of the Old Realms. Every city of that age lived on its stores, and a guild of wardens watched them, with cats to keep down the mice. Their patron was a small goddess of the storehouse, the Small Huntress, who has a cat's head in the old carvings.\n\nIn the Ashen Years the stores were all a city had. In the second year without a summer the mice and the rats bred in the granaries, and the wardens starved beside the grain they guarded. Their story is that they went to the Small Huntress and asked for two things: to keep the stores from the vermin, and to keep them from themselves. She gave both. The children born that winter had her ears and her tail. They heard a mouse through a wall, and they could not eat bread. The Catfolk call them the First Litter.\n\nA warden who cannot eat grain can be trusted with it. By the Catfolk account, the cities that came through the Ashen Years with their stores whole were the ones that kept such wardens, and the charters date from then. A grain town gives its Catfolk meat and fish, and they keep the town's stores. This is the Warden's Portion, and many towns of the Scattered Age still pay it.\n\nNot all of them stayed. When the Old Realms broke, most wardens had no city left to serve. They went out into the open grassland, where the game was, and became hunters. The two kinds, the wards of the towns and the bands of the grassland, still count themselves one people and marry between them."),
    };
}
